-- title:
--   Homothetic quaternionic period lattices and Fuchsian orbits
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and let $B=\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ be the associated quaternion algebra, and let $q,q'$ be primes. Assume `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`: $0<a$ or $0<b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the completed algebra $B\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ has all its nonzero elements invertible precisely when $q\in v$ or $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda\subseteq B$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule that is a maximal order, i.e. $1\in\Lambda$, $\Lambda$ is closed under multiplication, its $\mathbb{Q}$-span is all of $B$, it is finitely generated, and every order containing $\Lambda$ equals $\Lambda$. Let $\iota:B\to M_2(\mathbb{R})$ be an injective $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra homomorphism, and let $\tau,\tau'$ lie in the upper half-plane. Write $L_\sigma=$ `qmPeriodLattice ι Λ σ` for the image of $\Lambda$ under the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map $x\mapsto \iota(x)\cdot{}^t(\sigma,1)\in\mathbb{C}^2$. Then there exists $c\in\mathbb{C}$, $c\neq 0$, with $c\,L_\tau=L_{\tau'}$ if and only if there is $\gamma$ in `fuchsianGroup Λ ι` — the intersection of the image under $\iota$ of the subgroup of $B^\times$ generated by the units satisfying `IsUnitOf Λ` with the kernel of $\det:\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})\to\mathbb{R}^\times$ — such that $\gamma\cdot\tau=\tau'$. The proof uses the ramification hypothesis only to deduce $a\neq0$ and $b\neq0$, and uses only that $\Lambda$ is an order, not its maximality.
--
--   This is the level-one orbit relation for quaternionic period lattices: two points of the upper half-plane give homothetic lattices, that is isomorphic complex tori with $\Lambda$-multiplication, exactly when they lie in one orbit of the Fuchsian group attached to $\Lambda$ and $\iota$. It is the case of trivial level structure used in the description of the complex points of the Shimura curve, and is cited in the identification of isomorphism classes of fake elliptic curves with Hecke and Atkin–Lehner data at level one.
